  "cmpSpeedLegend": "Two standpoints per example: the filled badge judges the code — which version is nicer to write and read. The outlined ⚡ badge reports the measurement — the wall-clock winner at the largest benchmarked size, AOT-compiled; each page's Benchmark section has the full bars.",
  "@cmpSpeedLegend": { "description": "Legend line on the comparison index pages explaining the difference between the filled code-verdict badge and the outlined measured-speed badge. Keep the ⚡ character." },

  "cmpBenchMeta": "{cpu}, {ram} GB RAM · Dart {dart} (AOT-compiled) · {date}",
  "@cmpBenchMeta": { "description": "Machine line under the Benchmark heading. Keep every {placeholder} intact; they are replaced by the generator (CPU name, RAM, Dart version, date)." },

  "cmpBenchScale": "N = {n}",
  "@cmpBenchScale": { "description": "Heading of one benchmark scale block, e.g. 'N = 1,000,000'. Keep {n} intact; it is replaced by the generator with the formatted item count." },

  "cmpBenchNoneTiming": "No benchmark for this example. This operator is defined by elapsed time, not by work done: both sides wait out the same windows, so a bar would be measuring Dart's timers rather than either library. Shrinking the windows does not help either — how many values a burst produces would then depend on how fast the machine is, and the two sides could no longer be checked against an identical result. The examples built on timing operators are left unmeasured for that reason; the ones whose delays merely stand in for I/O are benchmarked with those delays set to zero.",
  "@cmpBenchNoneTiming": { "description": "Shown in place of the benchmark bars on a comparison example whose front matter sets `noBenchmark: timing` — an example built on time-defined operators (debounce, throttle, sample, race, and the interleaving-dependent combinators), where a measurement would time Dart's event loop rather than the libraries." },

  "cmpBenchAsyncNote": "Async case: the headline scale is N = {n}, not 1,000,000. Every element costs an event-loop turn on both sides, so a million real awaits would measure Dart's event loop for minutes — not the pipeline. Delays are zero-length and the example's concurrency limit is kept; what the bars compare is the pipeline machinery.",
  "@cmpBenchAsyncNote": { "description": "Disclaimer shown on async examples' Benchmark sections explaining why the headline N is capped (100,000, or lower for a case whose own algorithm is superlinear) instead of the sync cases' 1,000,000. Keep {n} intact; it is replaced with the case's headline N." },
  "cmpBenchMethodNote": "Bars are medians of repeated timed iterations in fresh processes per side (small N is batched for timer resolution). Sides within {margin}% of each other — or within {absMs} ms, a difference no person can perceive — count as a tie; close relative races are re-measured up to 5 runs. In an app, anything under a few milliseconds is invisible to the user regardless of which bar is shorter.",
  "@cmpBenchMethodNote": { "description": "Method footnote of the Benchmark section. Keep {margin} and {absMs} intact; they are replaced by the generator with the tie-margin percentage and the absolute tie floor in milliseconds." },

  "cmpBenchMemNote": "Memory is peak process RSS. The Dart VM and the dataset are identical on both sides, so the difference between the two bars is what the pipeline itself holds onto.",
  "@cmpBenchMemNote": { "description": "Footnote explaining how the memory bars should be read. RSS is a technical term (resident set size) — keep as 'RSS'." },

